{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And it was on the next day.</b> It was the day after Yom HaKippurim — thus we learned in Sifrei. And what is 'on the next day'? The day after his descent from the mountain. And perforce one can only say: the day after Yom HaKippurim — for before the giving of the Torah it is impossible to say 'and I shall make known the statutes, etc.', and from when the Torah was given until Yom HaKippurim Moshe did not sit to judge the people, for on the 17th of Tammuz he descended and broke the tablets, and the next day he went up early and stayed eighty days and descended on Yom HaKippurim. And this passage is not written in order — 'and it was on the next day' was not said until the second year — even according to the one who says Yitro came before the giving of the Torah, his dismissal to his land was only in the second year — for here it is said 'and Moshe sent his father-in-law', and we find in the march of the banners that Moshe said to him 'we are journeying to the place, etc.' (Numbers 10:29), 'do not leave us' (v. 31), and if this is before the giving of the Torah, once he sent him off and he went — where do we find that he returned? And if you say: there it does not say Yitro but Hovav, and it was the son of Yitro — Hovav is Yitro, for it is written 'of the children of Hovav, the father-in-law of Moshe' (Judges 4)." "<b>And Moshe sat, etc., and the people stood.</b> Sitting like a king, and all of them standing. And the matter weighed on Yitro, for he held the honor of Israel cheap, and he reproved him for this, as it is said (v. 14) 'Why do you sit alone while all the people stand about you.'" "<b>From morning until evening.</b> Is it possible to say so? Rather — any judge who renders a true judgment in truth, even for a single hour, Scripture accounts it upon him as though he were engaged in Torah all day, and as though he became a partner to the Holy One, blessed be He, in the work of Creation, for concerning it 'and it was evening, etc.' is said (Shabbat 10)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "Rashi uses the abbreviations אי\"א and יה\"כ; these are expanded in translation as 'impossible' and 'Yom HaKippurim' respectively."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:03.765317482Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}